  "prompt_mode": "short_sentence_continuation",
  "prompt_text": "Continue the text below with exactly one short, natural, coherent English sentence. Return only the continuation, with no preamble or explanation:\nWhen you're 28, science discovers a drug that stops all effects of aging, creating immortality. Your government decides to give the drug to all citizens under 26, but you and the rest of the “Lost Generations\" are deemed too high-risk. When you're 85, the side effects are finally discovered.\n\nThis is the story of how you, and the other Lost Generations, survive in a world where everyone younger than you never ages, but everyone older than you avoided the side effects.\n\nYou, personally, were lauded throughout your life for discovering the drug that stopped the effects of aging in a younger population, but the government decided, in principle, to deny you the chance to benefit from it.  \n\nYou were offered the drug from the black market but you refused.  You, and many others like you, were outraged at the government's decision, but some of the others took the drug anyway.  \n\nFor the most part, you just live your life as normal in this new, boring world where young celebrities never age, and never get replaced by younger counterparts.  \n\nEventually, the outrage about the unfair distribution of the drug dies down as well, and the older generations accept their fate.  \n\nIf anything, the economy thrives, because people no longer lose their vitality as they get older, and they have decades of wisdom and experience to apply to new projects.  \n\nYou and some of your generation are still nostalgic for the past, but you accept that a world without aging, even if just for a few, is better than the world that we had before.  Even though you won't be around to enjoy it forever, you are glad that many others will be.\n\nWhen you are halfway into your happy retirement, however, the side effects become clear.\n\nThis whole time you thought that everyone had stopped aging, but it turns out that they spent the decades aging very slowly.",
